# Yard data is personal data and does not go to a public remote.
#
# A yard record contains a street address, a rooftop-accurate latitude and
# longitude, a parcel identifier, and a written profile covering who lives
# there, when they travel and what their budget is. Any one of those identifies
# a household. Together they are a burglary brief.
#
# The rule is a whitelist, not a blacklist: everything is ignored, and the code
# is added back by name. A new yard directory is therefore ignored the moment it
# is created, without anyone having to remember to add it.
#
# To move yard data between machines, encrypt it first:
#     ./bin/yard vault lock <slug>       -> vault/<slug>.tar.gz.enc, safe to commit
#     ./bin/yard vault unlock <slug>     -> restores it on the other end
# Run `./bin/yard scrub` before any commit to check nothing leaked.

*

!.gitignore
!.gitattributes
!README.md
!AGENTS.md
!LICENSE
!requirements.txt

# The doubt gate. Checked in on purpose: a guardrail that lives only on one
# machine is not a guardrail, and this one has to survive a fresh clone.
!.cursor/
!.cursor/**

# Except the hook's debug trace. The payload carries a user email, a transcript
# path and every command the hook has judged, which is exactly the kind of thing
# a whitelist is supposed to keep out of a public remote.
.cursor/hooks/.debug
.cursor/hooks/payload.log

!lib/
!lib/**
!skills/
!skills/**
!agents/
!agents/**
!bin/
!bin/**
!tools/
!tools/**
!docs/
!docs/**

# Encrypted bundles only. The plaintext tarball never gets added.
# manifest.json is safe: dates and sizes, no street and no coordinate.
!vault/
!vault/*.enc
!vault/manifest.json
!vault/README.md

# Never, under any circumstances.
lib/**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
vault/*.tar.gz
vault/*.tar
.cache/
.DS_Store
